.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt EVP_KDF-PBKDF2 7ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm EVP_KDF-PBKDF2
.Nd The PBKDF2 EVP_KDF implementation
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Support for computing the
.Sy PBKDF2
password-based KDF through the
.Sy EVP_KDF
API.
.Pp
The EVP_KDF-PBKDF2 algorithm implements the PBKDF2 password-based key
derivation function, as described in SP800-132; it derives a key from a
password using a salt and iteration count.
.Pp
The output is considered to be a cryptographic key.
.Ss Identity
"PBKDF2" is the name for this implementation; it can be used with the
.Xr EVP_KDF_fetch 3
function.
.Ss Supported parameters
The supported parameters are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \(dqpass" ( Ns Sy OSSL_KDF_PARAM_PASSWORD Ns ) <octet string>
.It \(dqsalt" ( Ns Sy OSSL_KDF_PARAM_SALT Ns ) <octet string>
.It \(dqiter" ( Ns Sy OSSL_KDF_PARAM_ITER Ns ) <unsigned integer>
This parameter has a default value of 2048.
.It \(dqproperties" ( Ns Sy OSSL_KDF_PARAM_PROPERTIES Ns ) <UTF8 string>
.It \(dqdigest" ( Ns Sy OSSL_KDF_PARAM_DIGEST Ns ) <UTF8 string>
These parameters work as described in
.Xr EVP_KDF 3 .
.It \(dqpkcs5" ( Ns Sy OSSL_KDF_PARAM_PKCS5 Ns ) <integer>
This parameter can be used to enable or disable SP800-132 compliance
checks.
Setting the mode to 0 enables the compliance checks.
.Pp
The checks performed are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It - the iteration count is at least 1000.
.It - the salt length is at least 128 bits.
.It - the derived key length is at least 112 bits.
.El
The default provider uses a default mode of 1 for backwards
compatibility, and the FIPS provider uses a default mode of 0. This
option breaks FIPS compliance if it causes the approved "fips-indicator"
to return 0.
.It \(dqfips-indicator" ( Ns Sy OSSL_KDF_PARAM_FIPS_APPROVED_INDICATOR Ns ) <integer>
This option is used by the OpenSSL FIPS provider.
.Pp
A getter that returns 1 if the operation is FIPS approved, or 0
otherwise.
This may be used after calling EVP_KDF_derive.
It returns 0 if "pkcs5" is set to 1 and the derived key length, salt
length or iteration count test fails.
.El
.Sh NOTES
A typical application of this algorithm is to derive keying material for
an encryption algorithm from a password in the "pass", a salt in "salt",
and an iteration count.
.Pp
Increasing the "iter" parameter slows down the algorithm which makes it
harder for an attacker to perform a brute force attack using a large
number of candidate passwords.
.Pp
No assumption is made regarding the given password; it is simply treated
as a byte sequence.
.Sh CONFORMING TO
SP800-132
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr EVP_KDF 3 ,
.Xr EVP_KDF_CTX_new 3 ,
.Xr EVP_KDF_CTX_free 3 ,
.Xr EVP_KDF_CTX_set_params 3 ,
.Xr EVP_KDF_derive 3 ,
.Xr EVP_KDF 3
.Sh HISTORY
This functionality was added in OpenSSL 3.0.
